Description:

zeroskip: a new cyrusdb backend


Config changes:

A new backend called 'zeroskip' is added to most of the database engine
options in imapd.conf.  Unlike every other backend, a zeroskip database is a
directory rather than a single file: it is append-only, readers take no lock
at all, and compaction happens out of band.  It suits append-heavy workloads.

There's also a new option 'zeroskip_index_path'.  zeroskip caches pointer
tables, which speeds up opening a database that has accumulated unordered
files; by default the cache lives in a zeroskip.cache directory inside each
database, and this option moves it under a single directory shared by every
database, which can be a tmpfs.  Do not point it at a directory untrusted
users can write to, and do not point it at a database directory.

'cyrusdb_autoconvert' now also detects zeroskip databases, and conversion works
in both directions between zeroskip and the file-based backends.


Upgrade instructions:

No changes are required if you don't want to switch to zeroskip.  No defaults
have been changed.  If you do want to change, you can optionally set
cyrusdb_autoconvert, and also update the DB engine for individual database
types.

Note that anything which backs up or copies a Cyrus database by filename needs
to handle a directory for these databases.  'ctl_cyrusdb -c' now does, having
been fixed to remove directory-shaped backups when rotating.

